          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#experimental_observation"} Experimental observation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#eferencesTheoreticalPredictions"} Theoretical predictions
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesExperimentalObservation"} Experimental observation
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein"} Einstein
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"}> general relativity
            ”) predicts, via
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein's equations of motion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            may show a kind of oscillations of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            roughly analogous to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+waves"} electromagnetic waves
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            itself and do not depend on some “medium” such as a water wave does.) In fact, since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            is reflected in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+geometry"} pseudo-Riemannian geometry
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %h2#experimental_observation Experimental observation
          %p
            At the end of the 20th century, there had accumulated excellent but indirect evidence for gravitational waves from the observation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+pulsars"} binary pulsars
            (
            %a{:href => "#HulseTaylor75"}> Hulse-Taylor 75
            ). Their observed rotational motion loses energy precisely to the extent that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            predicts is being radiated away by gravitational waves.
          %p
            On 11 Febrary 2016 was the announcement of the first direct detection by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LIGO"} LIGO
            collaboration, using laser interferometry, of a gravitational wave signal coming from the in-spiral and merge of a pair of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes"} black holes
            (
            %a{:href => "#LIGO16"}> LIGO 2016
            ). The signal was a chirp at 35-250 Hz (converted to audio in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/TWqhUANNFXw"} this looped recording
          %p
            Further direct detections of gravitational wave events followed. The event GW170817
            %a{:href => "LIGOVirgo17"} LIGO-Virgo17
            showed gravitational waves from merging
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neutron+stars"} neutron stars
            coincident with the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+radiation"} electromagnetic radiation
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soft+graviton+theorem"} soft graviton theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+wave"} electromagnetic wave
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pp-wave+spacetime"}> pp-wave spacetime
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-wave"} M-wave
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radiation"} radiation
          %h2#References References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The first article that correctly derived gravitational waves from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            is
          %ul
            %li#Einstein18
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Über Gravitationswellen
          %p
            In particular this correctly stated that gravitational waves require a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadrupole+moment"} quadrupole moment
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/source"} source
            (e.g. a rotating
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+star+system"} binary star system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dipole+moment"} dipole moment
            (e.g. an oscillating charge) as for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+waves"} electromagnetic waves
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graviton"} graviton
            has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
            2, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photon"} photon
            has spin 1…), thereby correcting a mistake to this effect in the earlier article
          %ul
            %li#Einstein16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Einstein"}> Albert Einstein
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Näherungsweise Integration der Feldgleichungen der Gravitation
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://echo.mpiwg-berlin.mpg.de/ECHOdocuView?url=/permanent/echo/einstein/sitzungsberichte/BGG54UCY/index.meta&ww=0.7142&wh=0.7142&wx=0.2315"} web
          %p The reality of gravitational wave solutions however kept being a cause of concern for many years (Einstein himself was concerned that the linearization approximation used in their derivation might have been too coarse), for a brief account of the early history see
          %ul
            %li#Steinicke05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wolfgang+Steinicke"}> Wolfgang Steinicke
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Einstein and the Gravitational waves
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.hs.uni-hamburg.de/DE/GNT/events/pdf/steinicke05.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sean+Carroll"}> Sean Carroll
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "http://www.preposterousuniverse.com/blog/2005/09/16/einstein-vs-physical-review/"} Einstein vs. Physical Review
          %p
            A modern walk through the derivation of gravitational waves from linearization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            may be found for instance on pages 5-24 of
          %ul
            %li#Guidry13
              Mike Guidry,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://eagle.phys.utk.edu/guidry/astro616/"} Astrophysics 616: General Relativity, Black Holes, and Cosmology
              –
              %em Chapter 21 – Gravitational waves
              (
              %a{:href => "http://eagle.phys.utk.edu/guidry/astro616/lectures/lecture_ch21.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave"} Gravitational wave
          %p
            Review in view of modern gravitational wave detection (see
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesExperimentalObservation"} below
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Rong-Gen Cai et al.,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Gravitational-Wave Physics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.00187"} arXiv:1703.00187
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/nsr/nwx029"} doi:10.1093/nsr/nwx029
            %li
              %p
                Ligong Bian et al.,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Gravitational-Wave Physics II: Progress
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.10235"} arXiv:2106.10235
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/1869296"} spire:1869296
            %li
              %p
                Frans Pretorius,
                %em A Survey of Gravitational Waves
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.03797"}> arXiv:2306.03797
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simone+Speziale"}> Simone Speziale
                , Danièle A. Steer:
                %em An Introduction to Gravitational Wave Theory
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.21817"}> arXiv:2508.21817
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Discussion in view of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
          %ul
            %li
              Amaury Micheli, Patrick Peter,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum cosmological gravitational waves?
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Quantum+Gravity"} Handbook of Quantum Gravity
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.00182"} arXiv:2211.00182
          %h3#eferencesTheoreticalPredictions Theoretical predictions
          %p Discussion of theoretical predictions for events that have a chance to yield detectable gravitational wave signals includes:
          %ul
            %li#Damour10
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thibault+Damour"}> Thibault Damour
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Gravitational Waves from Coalescing Binary Black Holes: Theoretical and Experimental Challenges
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.theorie.physik.uni-muenchen.de/MATH/seminars/asc_kolloquium/archiv_sose10/damour/video_damour/index.html"} web
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hVy3YBalpWE"} youtube
          %p
            In particular, the computation of the signal from the coalescence of two inspiralling black hole
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+binary"} binaries
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#BuonannoDamour98
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandra+Buonanno"}> Alessandra Buonanno
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thibault+Damour"} Thibault Damour
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Effective one-body approach to general relativistic two-body dynamics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/9811091"} arXiv:gr-qc/9811091
            %li#BuonannoDamour00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandra+Buonanno"}> Alessandra Buonanno
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thibault+Damour"} Thibault Damour
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Binary black holes coalescence: transition from adiabatic inspiral to plunge
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0011052"} arXiv:gr-qc/0011052
            %li#DamourNagar
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thibault+Damour"}> Thibault Damour
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandro+Nagar"} Alessandro Nagar
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An improved analytical description of inspiralling and coalescing black-hole binaries
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0902.0136"} arXiv:0902.0136
            %li#Damour14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thibault+Damour"}> Thibault Damour
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandro+Nagar"} Alessandro Nagar
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A new analytic representation of the ringdown waveform of coalescing spinning black hole binaries
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1406.0401"} arXiv:1406.0401
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thibault+Damour"}> Thibault Damour
                ,
                %em
                  Radiative contribution to classical gravitational scattering at the third order in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.01641"}> arXiv:2010.01641
                )
          %p Review of the theoretical predictions and their experimental verification is given in
          %ul
            %li#Damour16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thibault+Damour"}> Thibault Damour
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gravitational Waves and Binary Systems
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qYong_D4VUI"} video recording
            %li#Damour19
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thibault+Damour"}> Thibault Damour
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the gravitational interaction of spinning bodies
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://souriau2019.fr/?page_id=2"} Souriau2019
                (
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8HO3DLKircs"}> video recording
                )
          %p
            Discussion using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+results+applied+elsewhere"} string theoretic
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KLT+relation"} KLT relation
            = succeed "-approach" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+copy"} double copy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+binary"} relativistic binary
            mergers for use with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LIGO"} LIGO
          %ul
            %li#BCRSSZ19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zvi+Bern"}> Zvi Bern
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+Cheung"} Clifford Cheung
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radu+Roiban"} Radu Roiban
              = succeed "," do
                %em Scattering Amplitudes and the Conservative Hamiltonian for Binary Systems at Third Post-Minkowskian Order
              %strong 122
              201603 (2019) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.04424"}> arXiv:1901.04424
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.122.201603"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.122.201603
          %p
            Discussion in relation to the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soft+graviton+theorem"} soft graviton theorem
          %ul
            %li
              Arnab Priya Saha, Biswajit Sahoo,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ashoke+Sen"} Ashoke Sen
              %em
                Proof of the Classical Soft Graviton Theorem in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D=4
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.06413"}> arXiv:1912.06413
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Discussion via tools from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://www.ias.edu/sns/s-matrix-marathon"} S-Matrix Marathon
              authors:
              %em Records from the S-Matrix Marathon: Gravitational Physics from Scattering Amplitudes
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.11649"}> arXiv:2412.11649
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#ReferencesExperimentalObservation Experimental observation
          %p
            Indirect detection of gravitational waves based on energy loss of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+pulsar"} binary pulsar
            system is due to
          %ul
            %li#HulseTaylor75
              Hulse, Taylor,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Discovery of a pulsar in a binary system
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://adsabs.harvard.edu/full/1975ApJ...195L..51H"} web
          %p
            The first proposal of the
            = succeed "-type" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LIGO"} LIGO
          %ul
            %li
              M. Gertsenshtein, I. Postivoit,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the detection of low frequency gravitational waves
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.jetp.ac.ru/cgi-bin/dn/e_016_02_0433.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Direct detection of gravitational waves by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LIGO"} LIGO
            experiment is reported in
          %ul
            %li#LIGO16
              %p
                B. P. Abbott et al. (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LIGO"} LIGO
                Scientific Collaboration and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Virgo"} Virgo
                Collaboration),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Observation of Gravitational Waves from a Binary Black Hole Merger
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.116.061102"} 10.1103/PhysRevLett.116.061102
            %li#LIGOVirgo17
              %p
                B. P. Abbott et al. (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LIGO"} LIGO
                Scientific Collaboration and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Virgo"} Virgo
                Collaboration),
                %em GW170817: Observation of Gravitational Waves from a Binary Neutron Star Inspiral
                Phys. Rev. Lett. 119, 161101, 2017 (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.119.161101"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.119.161101
                )
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Salvatore Vitale,
                %em The first five years of gravitational-wave astrophysics
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.03563"}> arXiv:2011.03563
                )
            %li
              %p
                Vitor Cardoso, Shauvik Biswas, Subhodeep Sarkar:
                %em The Physics of Black Holes and Their Environments: Consequences for Gravitational Wave Science
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.14841"}> arXiv:2511.14841
                \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
